l,"sequence_source":null,"smiles":null,"agentic_understanding":"Started from the saved identity-resolution snapshot, which already showed no candidate pool, no UniProt IDs, and no resolved sequence for Oncorhynchus mykiss EC 1.1.1.47. Minimal literature follow-up on PMID 1769217, PMID 7553351, and PMID 4397856 confirmed legacy rainbow trout liver glucose dehydrogenase assay and partial-purification studies, including a reported approximately 90 kDa monomer estimate, but no clone, accession, gene symbol, peptide sequence, or other unique sequence bridge. Because the paper context remains biochemical rather than sequence-identifying, the row stays manual_review_required.","critique_rationale":"The alternative would be to infer a modern trout glucose dehydrogenase identity from EC plus organism plus old liver biochemistry papers. That would overstate the evidence. The preserved batch evidence and the direct PMID follow-up never narrow the record to a unique accession-backed protein, so a conservative blocker is the defensible outcome.","audit_history":["Loop 1: Read the identity_resolution_snapshot first; confirmed sequence_resolved=false, no candidate matches, no UniProt IDs, and no preserved sequence source.","Loop 2: Reviewed the saved PMID set and fetched the linked PubMed records for 1769217, 7553351, and 4397856 to test whether any paper exposed accession-backed identity evidence.","Loop 3: Finalized manual_review_required after confirming the literature only supports trout liver glucose dehydrogenase activity and partial purification context, not a unique sequence assignment."],"verification_method":"selected_batch_payload_review","cofactor_name":"NADP+","assay_conditions_summary":"cofactor NADP+","paper_grounding_status":"review_excerpt_preserved","paper_grounding_source":"review_notes_excerpt","paper_grounding_note":"Verification notes preserve a cited paper excerpt from manual review.","abstract_identity_anchor_state":null,"abstract_identity_anchor_note":null